WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:13.259
Have you heard of a dragon whose tail releases
a spray of explosive slime?

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:17.189
Or a dragon that hunts with noxious fumes?

00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:21.460
Or one that vomits spires of super-heated
glass?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.250
Across various myths and cultures, dragons
have taken on many forms.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.410
But few are more fearsome than the terrors
that lurk within the pages of the Dragonslayer

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:38.010
Codex — a worldbuilding project from the
brilliant imagination of artist Sawyer Lee.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:43.940
The volume is written as a survival guide
to the world’s most dangerous dragons, explaining

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.570
their abilities, behavior, and how to slay
them if necessary.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.540
But be warned: many of these scaly tyrants
will not go down without a fight.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.180
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
learn critical things about each species that

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.540
can mean the difference between survival and
searing flames.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.810
And as usual, you can support the artist on
Patreon and follow the project using the links

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.040
in the description.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:19.729
Now, let’s dive into the burned pages, and
discover the secrets of the Dragonslayer Codex…

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:26.200
In the open plains, our first species of dragon
paints the land red with their terrible wrath.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:32.369
The nightmarish Scarlet Lashers are a threat
to anything that enters their domain.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.810
Lasher territory typically encompasses wide
spaces that allow full use of their whip-like

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.429
tail — which according to legend, can slice
a bison in half.

00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:49.049
As elegant as they are lethal, the agile Lashers
are known to move like malicious dancers.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:54.469
Their rust-colored eggs are a prized ingredient
in alchemy — and possess an unusual shape

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:56.709
to help them stay in their nest.

00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:00.159
And a Lasher’s nest is a grim sight indeed.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:06.369
During breeding season, Lashers construct
great ground nests composed of branches, bones,

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.429
and strewn corpses.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:13.900
These disturbing corpse-nests aren’t just
to protect eggs, but to show off the Lasher’s

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.040
skill to a potential mate.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:21.620
Throughout the mating period, the scales of
male Lashes will flush a deeper red than usual,

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:27.850
and they scream a dreadful call into the night,
which can be heard for several kilometers.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:33.950
Beyond mating calls, the multi-chambered airways
within the dragon’s crest amplify sound

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.780
to a lethal degree.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:41.980
As a result, slayers are advised to shield
their ears when pursuing a Scarlet Lasher.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:47.220
Yet perhaps the most terrifying feature of
the Lasher is their keen intelligence.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:53.210
A hunting style that involves outsmarting
small prey animals has resulted in an exceedingly

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.230
crafty species.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:03:00.090
Should you decide to venture into a Scarlet
Lasher’s territory, you must constantly

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:08.460
watch for traps, pitfalls, and other entanglements
of the dragon’s making…

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.430
Should you survive these traps and journey
to the dark islands just off the coast, you

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.450
would face another fascinating but frightening
species.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.760
One of the only known ‘flightless dragons,’
the Sicklemouth are a lineage infamous for

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.370
their sword-like jaws.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:30.210
They use these jaws to settle territorial
bouts with others of their own kind, and have

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:35.130
even been documented sparring with each other
— seemingly for practice.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.100
Even by human standards, the Sicklemouth is
an extremely adept swordsman.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:46.040
In combat, slayers report, their attacks resemble
those of trained professionals, not simply

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.760
wild beasts.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.400
Many explorers have been caught off guard
while charting these islands, and have met

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.740
their end underestimating the Sicklemouth.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.110
These clever beasts enjoy the challenge of
the hunt, so if you wish to shoo one off,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.180
it’s best to make your fighting seem boring.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.480
If you must tangle with a dragon, one weapon
a slayer should always have on hand is a Drakeprier.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:17.280
A kind of melee multitool, these versatile
devices can catch a dragon off guard if wielded

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.280
correctly.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.590
But since mishandling can result in the loss
of a finger, they should only be used by trained

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:27.360
professionals...

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.430
When crossing the expansive sands of the Dune
Sea, you and other unlucky travelers might

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.960
hear a series of choir-like calls.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.159
These aren’t the sounds of some lost musician,
but are the deceptively melodic cries of Wailing

00:04:43.159 --> 00:04:44.180
Bishops.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:50.740
A dragon subspecies common to desert environments,
Wailing Bishops are famous for their silver

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.560
scales, which reflect the harsh sunlight.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:58.850
These dragons can also vibrate their scales
while in flight, creating a mesmerizing hum

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.080
that adds to their chorus of sounds.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.889
Wailing Bishops feed by skimming the sands
of the great Dune Sea, snapping up any buried

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:10.779
subterranean lifeforms they come across.

00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:16.770
This ‘sand-skimming’ behavior is one of
the most unique across the dragon family tree.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:22.469
The Wailing Bishop’s primary food source
is the water-filled burrowing Dune Mole.

00:05:22.469 --> 00:05:27.850
These strange forms of life are also highly
sought after by humans who live in this region

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:33.240
as well — as they are a rare source of water
in this parched wasteland.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.330
While skimming for food, Wailing Bishops also
swallow excess sand.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:43.479
In the dragon’s stomach, the sand becomes
a goopy, molten material that Bishops then

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:48.240
regurgitate — forming spires of black glass
upon impact.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.169
The Bishops use these to create formidable
obsidian nests, which the dragons use as a

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:56.570
safe place to lay their eggs.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:02.580
But it’s not a place that’s safe for anyone
else…

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:09.180
Upon the stormy shoreline where the sea clashes
with the land, an unusual subspecies has become

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.070
a significant threat.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:17.139
All slayers would be wise to be wary of the
Tasseled Thundermaw, a semi-aquatic dragon

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:20.240
adapted for both the sky and the sea.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.039
Rippling with muscle and deceptively flexible,
the Thundermaw can be found wherever the water

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:28.560
is cold and the waves are high.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.300
The Thundermaw’s tail is the longest of
any dragon class, which continuously writhes

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.499
and coils subconsciously.

00:06:36.499 --> 00:06:40.150
Yet what is the purpose of a tail with a mind
of its own?

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.889
The answer might have to do with the Thundermaw’s
feeding strategy.

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.939
Some individuals have been seen “fishing”
with their flexible tails, which lure in fish

00:06:49.939 --> 00:06:51.580
with their wriggling movement.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.580
A question remains, however, as to whether
this is their primary hunting method, or just

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.659
a curious pastime.

00:06:59.659 --> 00:07:05.259
For Thundermaws can also hunt by sending a
short shock to stun their victims, before

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:07.969
lunging in to deliver the final blow.

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:14.180
It’s a powerful secret weapon, made possible
thanks to hidden organs in the head and neck

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.180
region.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.809
This attribute mirrors that of electric eels,
strange beasties known to use their electric

00:07:20.809 --> 00:07:24.419
fields both to subdue prey, and to locate
them.

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:29.830
It is likely the Thundermaws can use their
fields to locate prey as well, which would

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:33.249
explain how they always know when to strike…

00:07:33.249 --> 00:07:40.569
May be best to leave these dragons to the
professional slayers...

00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:46.199
When walking amongst the dense trees of the
region known as the Blood Forest, a slayer

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:49.960
should always keep one eye turned upwards.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:56.309
For the fast and agile Gaping Kilnneck soar
just above the canopy, ready to snatch prey

00:07:56.309 --> 00:07:59.159
with their interlocking teeth.

00:07:59.159 --> 00:08:04.680
Kilnnecks are light-bodied dragons, easily
identifiable by their slim form, which is

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:10.120
perfect for maneuvering the densely forested
environments where they can be found.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:15.189
Their beautiful, frilled tail likewise helps
break the silhouette of this dragon against

00:08:15.189 --> 00:08:17.840
the foliage in the Blood Forest.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.399
Yet the most notable feature of the Kilnneck
is their sheer firepower.

00:08:22.399 --> 00:08:28.619
While Kilnnecks themselves aren’t the largest
species, their fiery breath rivals that of

00:08:28.619 --> 00:08:30.400
even the mightiest dragons.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:36.180
To produce such a massive wall of fire, like
a common serpent, these dragons can unhinge

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.070
their jaws and swing them forwards.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:45.040
Aside from their signature flame barrage,
Kilnnecks are ambush predators that hunt using

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.630
their immense speed.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:52.620
Observant slayers have recorded them flying
low over the countryside, snatching up anything

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.340
from the size of a fish… to the size of
a cow.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:01.180
But even the most dangerous animal’s life
is more than just hunting.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:06.100
When rearing their young, these dragons take
a break from fire-breathing to carry their

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:09.430
brood in their unhinged jaws.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:14.020
Juvenile Kilnnecks have a relatively good
chance at making it to adulthood compared

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:20.070
to other dragons — meaning the Blood Forest
is teeming with full grown fire-breathers.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:27.660
Only the most courageous—or foolhardy—would
venture in.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:32.880
On nights when the skies are darkest, slayers
who wish to see the sunrise again must be

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.330
on their guard.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:39.280
A secretive and cunning dragon subspecies
prowls in the shadows.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:44.940
Black Blisterwhips are so dreaded that just
seeing one is considered a bad omen.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:48.600
Their dark scales help them vanish into the
night sky.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.440
Unlike most dragons, they prefer to feed on
bone marrow, and possess molar-like teeth

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:57.220
to crack open the bones of their prey.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:02.810
Like bats, Blisterwhips are known to be nocturnal
— only hunting at night, and retreating

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.500
to their dark caves when the sun rises.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:11.270
And the Blisterwhip’s most deadly ability
is something impossible to anticipate.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:17.510
When a Blisterwhip swings their tail, it secrets
a highly flammable green slime, which the

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:21.840
dragon then ignites — producing a sudden
wall of searing flame.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.150
It’s one of the most extreme attacks of
any dragon.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:31.840
When a dragon has a slayer on the ropes, a
slayer’s best friend is a Kinghound.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.830
These giant canines are bred to track and
pursue dragons, with their calloused, hairless

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:41.680
bodies far less flammable than an ordinary
dog’s.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:47.660
Deeply loyal, they’ll bravely protect their
owners against even the most fearsome of foes.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:53.390
Best to bring one along if you’re perusing
a Blisterwhip.

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:59.180
In the sun-scorched wastes of a region known
as the Gorgelands, heatstroke isn’t the

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.800
only danger.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:07.010
Ruling over the labyrinthian ravines are the
ill-mannered Hellbaskers, creatures just as

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:10.670
unpleasant as the environment they inhabit.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:15.730
Hellbasker flames are the hottest of any dragon,
and are used to bore the tunnels in which

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:16.980
they nest.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:23.370
Their eggs are long and thin, able to be hidden
deep inside rocky crags and crevices within

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.790
the Hellbasker’s territory.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:31.050
While the flames of adult Hellbaskers are
hot enough to melt shields and swords in a

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.160
flash, the flames of Hellbasker chicks aren’t
nearly as formidable.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:40.830
The size of small terriers, the sand-toned
wyrmlings are a far cry from the tyrants they

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.330
grow into.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:49.030
When hunting, adult Hellbaskers pursue quarry
into the narrowest of canyons, able to pitch

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:53.110
and control their aerial maneuvers to the
most precise degree.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:59.530
And such flexibility is necessary when chasing
their preferred prey: Armorheads.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:05.730
Large, heavily defended herbivores related
to wild boars, Armorhead have well-developed

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.810
neck muscles and ever-growing tusks to fend
off Hellbasker attacks.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:16.260
The Armorhead’s thick skin often bears the
scars of attempted predation, which helps

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.530
determine their status within their pack.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:28.100
Clearly, slayers aren’t the only group who
have had some success fending off dragons...

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:33.740
At the edge of settlements, one species is
known to feast on discarded scraps.

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:39.170
The aggressive Rattle Dreads are scavengers
that seek out small towns for their trash

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.190
piles.

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:45.600
Led by the best sense of smell in the known
world, they forage for anything they can fit

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.100
into their huge maw.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:53.560
And anything the Rattle Dread doesn’t eat
they regurgitate out in fiery scattershot,

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:59.090
with this species contributing to the highest
number of dragon attacks to date.

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:03.990
Rattle Dreads are known to incubate their
eggs in their previous prey, making them a

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.500
much-reviled species.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:11.770
Once they hatch from their unwilling hosts,
young Rattle Dreads grow quickly, gaining

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:15.560
almost four times their mass in muscle in
just a few months.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:20.460
But to reach such sizes requires a tremendous
amount of food.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:26.050
These hungry beasts aren’t afraid to enter
barns and homes in search of a meal, and are

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.370
completely confident around humans.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:33.410
You’ll be warned they are coming by the
rattling of their mane of hollow spikes — a

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.060
sound that signals hungry mouths in the dark.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.140
By the time you hear it, though, it may already
be too late.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:42.290
Some Rattle Dreads have even been known to
play dead in the hopes a carless human will

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:43.810
approach.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:50.440
One way or another, these scavengers get their
meal…

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:55.430
Within the mossy woodland, a unique foe turns
the land into a minefield.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:01.720
The Emerald Bombardier is a relatively small
dragon with a relatively large trick up its

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.720
sleeve.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:08.800
While perched high up in the trees, Bombardiers
build fortified nests of hardened saliva.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:14.440
Glowing chunks of this ‘Dragonstone’ sometimes
fall to the earth and explode under enough

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:20.280
pressure — a trait that makes Bobardier
saliva a highly sought-after weapon of war.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:25.570
But Bombardiers don’t give up their saliva
so easily, and defend their forests to the

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:26.570
end.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:31.170
And there is another curious lifeform in these
temperate forests.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:37.240
The Inusitherium are large herbivores that
tower over Emerald Bombardiers, and show that

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:41.060
dragons aren’t the only expectational creatures
in this world.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:46.780
Their unique, modified hooves aid them in
lowering branches, and in defending themselves

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.160
from predators.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:53.070
Their hardy and self-sufficient nature means
that they make highly prized domesticated

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:59.730
animals to the scattered human populations
that dwell here…

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:05.510
Upon the weathered mountains of the coast,
a seldom-seen creature glides upon the thermals.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:12.720
A rare, solitary species, the Veilwright is
a dragon lineage with highly unusual features.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:18.360
Their multiple nostrils churn out smoke wherever
they go, suffocating attackers and veiling

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.900
themselves in a dark cloud.

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:26.680
Thanks to their smoking camouflage, few Slayers
have ever seen a Veilwright in full.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:33.080
Likewise, these dragons keep their eggs in
foggy, hidden nests high in the mountains.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:38.030
When Veilwrights chicks are first born, they
latch on to their parents for warmth, clinging

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:42.460
to their bodies for a period that can last
several months.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:47.390
Veilwrights feed upon the poisonous vegetation
that flourishes in their native habitat.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:53.630
And these dragons are capable of isolating
and weaponizing the deadly chemicals within,

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:56.560
fusing it with their smoking shroud.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:02.090
Different plants have different effects: with
Lone-wart causing temporary amnesia, Deadleaf

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:07.620
causing neural shutdown, and Throat Oak causing
the esophagus to close.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:12.930
Even if a Veilwright hasn’t recently ingested
poison, their veil of smoke is a powerful

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.940
tool for distraction.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:19.420
Even an experienced slayer might struggle
to tell where a Veilwright is within their

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:23.550
cloud — uncertainty which can often prove
fatal.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:29.390
Many accomplished slayers have met their untimely
end underestimating species like the Veilwright

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:35.520
— whose size suggests an easy mark, but
whose arsenal of abilities can overpower most

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.520
any foe.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:44.230
It is best to avoid confrontation with these
dragons entirely...

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:50.690
In the frozen wastes of the far north, the
slopes echo with the call of a great behemoth.

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:56.630
One of the heaviest dragon lineages, the Hullbreakers
physique reflects their extreme lifestyle.

00:16:56.630 --> 00:17:02.290
They have developed a novel adaptation to
the harsh ice of their habitat: pummeling

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:04.060
it to mist.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:09.140
With their enormous forelimbs, Hullbreakers
can smash through the sheets of ice that sources

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.190
of food swim beneath.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:16.660
Their eggs are kept warm in pouches beneath
the dragon’s wings, which reduces the risk

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:19.730
of losing them to the surrounding ice.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:25.250
Once a juvenile hatches, they are dangerous
even in their youth — often towering over

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:27.620
adult slayers.

00:17:27.620 --> 00:17:33.740
Juvenile Hullbreakers have been known to inhabit
small, frosty ruins before striking out into

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.850
the snowy wilderness.

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:41.490
With their devastating striking ability, an
adult Hullbreaker is not a force to be taken

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:46.150
lightly, as their fists can smash through
more than just the ice.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:52.220
With such oversize forelimbs, one might assume
the Hullbreaker would be entirely flightless.

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:57.790
Yet despite their weight, Hullbreakers are
capable of gliding short distances, with their

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:01.820
immense punching force making for excellent
launches.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:07.340
Just as wild and unforgiving as the snows
they tread upon, Hullbreakers have singlehandedly

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:11.110
caused the loss of many an aspiring crew.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:16.820
With all their incredible power, this is a
species that only the most qualified slayers

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:21.080
should contend with.

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.410
In the highest mountains of the known world,
one of the most dangerous species of all rules

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.730
over the skies.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:34.630
An avalanche of muscle and horn, the Great
Kinsbane has unmatched aggression and sheer

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:35.720
strength.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:41.480
Most stories concerning castle guarding dragons
are inspired by these beasts, who live wherever

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:42.700
they please.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:48.050
Indeed, no human slayer has ever successfully
managed to defeat one.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:53.240
The eggs of the Great Kinsbane are as tough
as the beasts themselves, with shells that

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:55.590
resemble balls of iron.

00:18:55.590 --> 00:19:00.540
Their hatchlings, likewise, emerge bulky and
well developed — although they need the

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:04.370
help of a parent to break through the iron-like
shell.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:09.580
Great Kinsbane have been known to sleep for
an entire month while their eggs incubate.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:15.180
But even a sleeping Kinsbane can still be
a serious threat to anything that creeps too

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:16.360
close.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:21.330
Only a fool would test their luck around such
dangerous forms of life.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:26.780
With all that slayers have learned about dragons;
it is easy to feel that humanity should no

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:30.050
longer cower in fear when they fly overhead.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:35.230
But in the case of the Great Kinsbanes, a
little cowering is probably a good idea…

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:44.490
The Dragonslayer Codex is a survival guide
to a land torn between humans and dragons.

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:47.940
Yet perhaps things won’t always be this
way.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:52.780
Within the world of the codex, some slayers
are making an effort to better coexist with

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:58.500
these creatures — and perhaps one day form
alliances with the incredible beasts.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:03.620
Sawyer Lee hopes to publish the codex as a
physical book in the future, and if that’s

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:08.110
something you’re interested in, remember
you can follow and support the project using

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:09.860
the links in the description.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:15.560
A follow up volume, the Drakonry, is also
planned that will explore the possibility

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.800
of things like Dragon riding — so stay tuned!

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:23.179
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:20:23.179 --> 00:20:28.250
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:32.950
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:42.240
See you in the next video.

